-## Subtopics
-
-### T-NeRF
-A variant that represents the scene with a 6D input $(x, y, z, \theta, \phi, t)$ directly without considering an intermediate canonical configuration [S1]. It generally produces less satisfying results than D-NeRF because it does not effectively exploit temporal redundancy [S1].
-
-### Neural Volumes (NV)
-A method for generating 3D images and videos using deep learning based on representing 3D scenes as a set of volumetric data (e.g., voxels) [S2]. D-NeRF is compared against NV [S2].
-
-### Scene Representation Networks (SRN)
-A type of deep learning model used to generate 3D images and videos of scenes, aiming for high-quality consistency with input data [S2]. D-NeRF is compared against SRN [S2].
+**Parameters:**
+*   **Canonical Time:** $t=0$ [S1].
+*   **Positional Encoding Depth:** $L=10$ for $x$, $L=4$ for $d$ and $t$ [S1, S2].
+*   **Network Depth:** 8 layers for both $\Psi_x$ and $\Psi_t$ [S1].
+*   **Training Images:** $400 \times 400$ resolution [S1].
+*   **Iterations:** 800,000 [S1].
+*   **Batch Size:** $N_s = 4096$ rays [S1].
+*   **Samples per Ray:** 64 [S1].
+*   **Optimizer:** Adam, learning rate $5 \times 10^{-4}$, $\beta_1=0.9$, $\beta_2=0.999$, exponential decay to $5 \times 10^{-5}$ [S1].
+*   **Curriculum Learning:** Input images are sorted by timestamp, and images with higher timestamps are incrementally added to improve convergence [S1].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Naive Extension Failure:** Directly mapping $(x, y, z, t)$ to density/radiance does not produce satisfying results because it fails to exploit temporal redundancy [S1].
+*   **Single View Per Time:** The method is designed for sparse monocular views (one view per time instant). It does not assume multiple views per time instance [S1].
+*   **Canonical Anchor:** The choice of $t=0$ as the canonical configuration is arbitrary but necessary to enforce $\Psi_t(x, 0) = 0$ [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The method uses two 8-layer MLPs. The computational cost is dominated by the forward pass of these networks for each sample along each ray.
-*   **Performance:** On the 'Hell Warrior' scene, D-NeRF achieves MSE $31 \times 10^{-4}$, PSNR 23.19, SSIM 0.93, LPIPS 0.08, compared to NeRF's MSE $44 \times 10^{-3}$, PSNR 13.52, SSIM 0.81, LPIPS 0.25 [S1]. On the 'T-Rex' scene, D-NeRF achieves MSE $5 \times 10^{-4}$, PSNR 32.80, SSIM 0.98, LPIPS 0.03, compared to NeRF's MSE $21 \times 10^{-3}$, PSNR 18.19, SSIM 0.89, LPIPS 0.14 [S1].
-*   **Training Time:** Trained for 800k iterations on 400x400 images, batch size 4096 rays, 64 samples per ray, on a single Nvidia GTX 1080 for 2 days [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Can handle non-rigid deformations, articulated motion, and rigid motion from a single view per time instant [S1]. Produces complete 3D meshes as a side product [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a sparse set of images; performance may degrade if the canonical configuration ($t=0$) is not representative or if deformations are extreme relative to the canonical state [G]. Naive extension (T-NeRF) fails to capture temporal redundancy effectively [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training requires 800k iterations. On a single Nvidia GTX 1080, training takes approximately 2 days [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Performance:**
+    *   **Hell Warrior Scene:** MSE $31 \times 10^{-4}$, PSNR 23.19, SSIM 0.93, LPIPS 0.08. (Comparison: NeRF MSE $44 \times 10^{-3}$, PSNR 13.52) [S1].
+    *   **T-Rex Scene:** MSE $5 \times 10^{-4}$, PSNR 32.80, SSIM 0.98, LPIPS 0.03. (Comparison: NeRF MSE $21 \times 10^{-3}$, PSNR 18.19) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles rigid, articulated, and non-rigid motions. Can synthesize novel views at arbitrary times from sparse monocular inputs without ground-truth geometry [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on a single view per time instant, which may limit geometric consistency compared to multi-view methods. Performance depends heavily on the quality of the canonical space alignment [S1].
 
 ## Background
-D-NeRF was proposed by Albert Pumarola, Enric Corona, Gerard Pons-Moll, and Francesc Moreno-Noguer [S1]. The method was presented at CVPR 2021 (arXiv:2011.13961v1, Nov 2020) [S1]. It builds upon the Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) framework [26] and generalizes it to handle dynamic, non-rigid scenes [S1]. The approach is inspired by classical 3D scene flow [44] and aims to exploit temporal redundancy by mapping deformed scenes to a canonical space [S1].
+D-NeRF was proposed by Albert Pumarola, Enric Corona, Gerard Pons-Moll, and Francesc Moreno-Noguer from the Institut de Robòtica i Informàtica Industrial (CSIC-UPC) and the Max Planck Institute for Informatics [S1]. It was published in CVPR 2021 (arXiv:2011.13961v1, Nov 27, 2020) [S1]. The method builds on NeRF [26] and generalizes it to handle non-rigid scenes. It differs from T-NeRF, which uses a 6D input $(x, y, z, \theta, \phi, t)$ directly without a canonical mapping [S1]. It is compared against Neural Volumes (NV), Scene Representation Networks (SRN), and Layered Light Field Fusion (LLFF) [S2].
 
